WORK TASK — Mission Control Work Model/Effort Contract Enforcement

Objective

Complete the Astra/Sol routing optimization by making Mission Control carry and enforce the model-and-effort selection already defined in the merged Universal policy.

Canonical policy:
patterns/work-model-and-effort-routing.md

Current merged policy commit:
fc3d0d7592a4fa69e94ff8ae31d9a4e5433b73cb

Current runtime:
tools/codex-mission-control/restored/codex-mission-control/

The previous optimization established:
MISSION_CONTROL_MODEL_ROUTER_HOOK_MISSING

This task fills that narrow gap. It does not redesign routing policy, invent a scheduler, benchmark models, change subscriptions, spend credits, or alter Chat/Work authority.

Chat has already selected the routing architecture. Work is executing the bounded implementation residue.

Reasoning budget

Use the lowest Work reasoning level expected to implement and debug this bounded runtime/schema change successfully under the merged routing policy.

Run the normal Work-side model/effort preflight. Record the actual model/effort if observable; do not fabricate it.

Governing invariants

Preserve all current Universal and nested Mission Control instructions.

Especially:

Chat owns routing policy and semantic decisions.

Work executes only the bounded implementation.

patterns/work-model-and-effort-routing.md is authoritative for model/effort selection.

patterns/chat-work-execution-routing-threshold.md remains the Work-admission boundary.

patterns/runtime-chat-work-authority-admission-and-internal-routing.md remains the runtime authority gate.

consumer-seam acceptance remains the correctness boundary.

no purchased credits or nonzero API spend.

no deployment in this task.

no automatic merge by Work.

Do not weaken current source-bound reasoning, owner-zero-spend, internal-routing, finalization, or GitHub-receipt controls.

Problem to solve

Mission Control can authorize EXECUTE_BOUNDED_TASK, but the runtime request does not carry an enforceable selected Work model/effort contract.

The canonical Chat-to-Codex directive has workReasoningBudget, but it does not bind:

selected model;

exact selected effort;

routing tier;

routing triggers;

Fast-mode state;

policy identity.

The execution receipt also does not bind the requested profile to the profile actually observed at execution.

Therefore Mission Control cannot currently distinguish:

Chat requested Sol Medium and Work actually ran Sol Medium;

Chat requested Astra Low and Work ran another model;

the requested effort was not selectable;

the model/effort was unobservable;

the runner silently used a default.

Fix that.

Phase 1 — Establish actual execution-surface capability

Before changing runtime behavior, inspect the current installed/active Work execution path and existing Mission Control/browser automation.

Answer with evidence:

Can the current automated Work launch surface explicitly select GPT-5.6 Sol vs GPT-6 Astra?

Can it explicitly select the supported reasoning effort?

Can it read back or otherwise independently verify the effective model after selection?

Can it independently verify effective effort?

Can it determine Fast mode state?

Are these controls available through an API/structured surface, or only UI/browser controls?

Does the current vps-browser-relay or another existing component already contain reusable model/thinking control primitives?

Use current local product behavior and current official OpenAI docs where needed.

Do not run a substantive model task merely to discover controls if a non-task probe or UI/config inspection is enough.

Do not spend credits.

Capability classification

Classify each of model, effort, and fastMode as:

SET_AND_VERIFY

SET_ONLY

VERIFY_ONLY

UNOBSERVABLE

Do not invent capability.

If exact model or effort cannot be both set and verified on the current automated Work execution surface, continue implementing contract propagation and fail-closed/advisory semantics below, but do not claim end-to-end enforcement is active.

Phase 2 — Add canonical execution-model contract

Prefer one shared typed definition in Mission Control rather than duplicate string fields.

Suggested semantic shape; adapt names to current conventions without changing meaning:

type WorkModel = "GPT_5_6_SOL" | "GPT_6_ASTRA";

type WorkEffort =
  | "LOW"
  | "MEDIUM"
  | "HIGH"
  | "XHIGH"
  | "MAX";

type WorkRoutingTier =
  | "SOL_LOW"
  | "SOL_MEDIUM"
  | "ASTRA_LOW"
  | "ASTRA_MEDIUM"
  | "ASTRA_HIGH"
  | "ASTRA_XHIGH"
  | "ASTRA_MAX"
  | "SOL_HIGH_EXCEPTION";

interface WorkExecutionProfile {
  model: WorkModel;
  effort: WorkEffort;
  routingTier: WorkRoutingTier;
  routingTriggers: string[];
  fastMode: boolean;
  policyRef: "patterns/work-model-and-effort-routing.md";
  policyCommit: string;
}

Do not assume every effort exists for every surface. Runtime validation must distinguish invalid policy combinations from unavailable product controls.

Cross-field validation

At minimum enforce:

SOL_LOW -> Sol + Low

SOL_MEDIUM -> Sol + Medium

SOL_HIGH_EXCEPTION -> Sol + High

ASTRA_LOW -> Astra + Low

ASTRA_MEDIUM -> Astra + Medium

Astra High/XHigh/Max tiers -> matching Astra effort

fastMode defaults false and true must be explicit

routingTriggers must be non-empty for Astra Low+ and Sol High exception

exception/high tiers require a non-empty reason/trigger

policy reference/version must be present

Do not encode volatile plan quotas or prices as runtime validity requirements.

Phase 3 — Bind the Chat-authored directive

Update the canonical execution directive schema/template so a nontrivial Work execution carries:

workExecutionProfile

alongside the existing workReasoningBudget.

Preserve backward compatibility only where genuinely required by existing fixtures/runtime.

Preferred migration:

bump schema version if current schema semantics require it;

new directives created after this patch must contain the profile;

old durable artifacts can still parse through an explicit legacy path if current recovery requires it;

legacy absence must never be silently interpreted as a specific model.

Use a typed explicit state such as:

LEGACY_MODEL_PROFILE_UNSPECIFIED

when recovering old artifacts.

Do not fabricate Sol or default effort for legacy artifacts.

Phase 4 — Admission enforcement

Extend the existing bounded-execution admission path:

POST /api/worker-channel/{worker}/admission

and its current ChatWorkAuthorityRequest / supervision runtime parsing so an EXECUTE_BOUNDED_TASK request carries the source-bound workExecutionProfile.

The profile is execution authority supplied by the verified Chat directive; Work may not rewrite it.

Admission must validate:

source receipt remains valid;

bounded execution remains valid;

execution scope remains Work-eligible;

model profile is structurally valid;

model/profile combination matches the canonical policy shape;

no spend permission is implied by model choice.

The authority gate must not decide which model is strategically best; Chat already did that.

Admission output

When execution is admitted, return the exact authorized profile in the admission result so the runner can consume it without reinterpreting prose.

Suggested semantic state:

{
  "mayExecute": true,
  "authorizedWorkExecutionProfile": { }
}

Do not weaken existing fields.

If profile is missing on a new-format directive/request:

REJECT_MISSING_WORK_EXECUTION_PROFILE

If malformed/inconsistent:

REJECT_INVALID_WORK_EXECUTION_PROFILE

Do not route those failures to a more expensive model.

Phase 5 — Pre-launch model/effort enforcement

Implement the narrowest adapter at the existing Work launch/control boundary.

Do not invent an alternate runner if an existing Work/browser control can set the requested model and effort.

Before substantive execution:

receive the authorized profile directly from admission;

set model exactly when capability = SET_AND_VERIFY or SET_ONLY;

set effort exactly when supported;

set Fast mode to the authorized boolean;

verify effective settings where capability permits;

persist a preflight record before substantive task execution.

Fail-closed behavior

When the requested profile is materially enforceable but observed settings disagree:

WORK_EXECUTION_PROFILE_MISMATCH

Do not launch the task.

Examples:

requested Astra Low, observed Sol;

requested Medium, observed High;

Fast requested false, observed true.

When the surface cannot verify a field:

never claim it matched;

record exact capability state;

apply the merged Universal policy's existing observability/materiality rule.

For an execution where model/effort identity is materially decision-relevant and cannot be verified, fail closed:

WORK_EXECUTION_PROFILE_UNVERIFIABLE

For simple deterministic execution where the current Universal policy genuinely permits proceeding despite an unobservable level, record:

PROFILE_FIELD_UNOBSERVABLE_PROCEEDED_BY_POLICY

Do not create a new owner prompt merely because a field is unobservable unless the current policy makes it materially decision-relevant.

Phase 6 — Receipt/finalization binding

Extend the canonical execution receipt with a factual model-routing section.

It must distinguish:

requested profile;

authorized profile returned by admission;

effective/observed profile;

per-field observability;

preflight result;

mismatch/unverifiable state;

escalation during task, if any;

final successful profile;

Fast mode;

relevant allowance delta if exposed;

routing telemetry eligibility/index.

Suggested semantics:

{
  "workExecution": {
    "requestedProfile": {},
    "authorizedProfile": {},
    "observedProfile": {
      "model": "... or null",
      "effort": "... or null",
      "fastMode": false
    },
    "observability": {
      "model": "SET_AND_VERIFY",
      "effort": "SET_AND_VERIFY",
      "fastMode": "SET_AND_VERIFY"
    },
    "preflight": "MATCH | MISMATCH | UNVERIFIABLE | PARTIAL",
    "escalations": [],
    "finalProfile": {},
    "allowanceDelta": null
  }
}

Adapt to current schemas.

Finalization gate

The finalization path must reject a receipt claiming successful bounded execution when:

admission authorized one profile and the receipt proves a different effective profile;

a required-verification field is unverified;

the receipt omits the new model profile for a new-format directive;

an escalation occurred without a new source-bound authorization when the canonical policy requires Chat to select the changed tier.

Do not let Work self-escalate from Sol to Astra or Low to Medium by merely changing its receipt.

A model/effort escalation is a new execution profile and must be source-bound to an authorized Chat decision/directive.

Mechanical retries at the same authorized profile remain allowed when already within task scope.

Phase 7 — 10-task telemetry window

Activate the prospective telemetry contract from:

templates/WORK-MODEL-ROUTING-TELEMETRY.json

for the next 10 eligible nontrivial Work executions after the runtime patch becomes active.

Do not run synthetic tasks merely to fill the window.

Each finalized eligible execution should make it possible to populate:

task/repo identifier;

residual execution class;

requested model/effort/tier;

routing triggers;

direct consumer-seam result;

failure classification;

wall time;

retries/interventions;

test wall time if available;

allowance delta if exposed;

escalation;

final successful tier;

hindsight classification: appropriate / clearly overpowered / clearly underpowered.

Storage

Use the smallest durable mechanism consistent with current Mission Control architecture.

Prefer existing event/receipt persistence over inventing another database.

Do not write prompts, secrets, source content, or private user data into routing telemetry.

Checkpoints

after eligible task 5: create/emit the required concise local summary; no automatic policy change;

after eligible task 10: produce a durable review artifact/event for Chat;

do not mutate the routing policy automatically.

Phase 8 — UI/dashboard visibility

Only add minimal visibility needed to debug enforcement.

If the dashboard already has a worker/task detail surface, expose compact fields such as:

requested model/effort;

observed model/effort;

preflight match state;

routing tier;

telemetry progress N/10.

Do not redesign the UI.

Do not make model routing an owner choice that requires routine clicking.

The route is selected by Chat policy; the dashboard is observability, not a new manual control panel.

Security and privacy

Model-routing telemetry must not include:

prompt bodies;

credential/environment values;

private source text;

tokens/secrets;

full local filesystem paths unless already public/non-sensitive and required for debugging.

Use IDs, enums, hashes, durations, and bounded non-sensitive reason codes.

No model choice may imply permission to inspect secrets.

Tests

Use focused/affected tests during implementation; full checkpoint only where current repository policy requires it.

Add regression coverage for at least:

valid Sol Low contract admitted;

valid Sol Medium contract admitted;

valid Astra Low contract admitted;

invalid tier/model mismatch rejected;

Astra trigger requirement enforced;

Sol High exception requires trigger;

missing new-format profile rejected;

legacy artifact remains explicitly unspecified, not silently defaulted;

admission returns exact authorized profile;

observed profile mismatch blocks launch/finalization;

unobservable material profile fails closed;

simple deterministic unobservable case follows existing policy without fabricating observation;

Work cannot self-escalate model/effort without new source-bound authorization;

Chat-plan/access/context/mechanical failure does not auto-escalate;

Fast mode false is enforced by default;

telemetry record contains no prompt/source/secret fields;

eligible telemetry counts toward 10; trivial/non-Work runs do not;

task-5 and task-10 checkpoint behavior does not mutate policy automatically;

existing Chat/Work authority tests remain green;

existing final-response/finalization tests remain green.

Consumer-seam acceptance for this task:

A source-bound Chat directive selecting a model/effort profile must either cause the exact profile to be selected and truthfully verified for Work execution, or the task must fail closed with an explicit unverified/mismatch state. No Work execution may silently run under a different or assumed profile.

An upstream schema-only test is not sufficient.

Runtime/deployment boundary

This task may modify the Mission Control source/runtime inside the Universal repository and open a PR.

It may not:

merge the PR;

deploy Mission Control;

restart production services;

change subscription/account settings;

purchase credits;

change active owner zero-spend policy.

Chat will inspect the PR and decide merge/deployment.

Required artifacts

Return:

MISSION CONTROL WORK MODEL CONTRACT IMPLEMENTED

with:

capability matrix for current Work model/effort/Fast controls;

exact files changed;

directive schema change;

admission contract change;

launch/preflight enforcement path;

receipt/finalization enforcement path;

telemetry persistence path;

tests and results;

PR link;

exact head SHA;

whether end-to-end enforcement is:

ENFORCED_SET_AND_VERIFY

PARTIALLY_ENFORCED

CONTRACT_ONLY_SURFACE_UNVERIFIABLE

any remaining product-surface limitation;

direct artifact bundle link/attachment.

Do not merge or deploy.
